<%# One game on the league scoreboard. Locals: game (Game).

    Every color on this tile is the TEAM'S OWN brand color, read through
    Team#card_background / #card_mascot so a light-field team (gold Saints, red
    Bucs) gets its palette the right way round rather than a hardcoded dark
    assumption. The leading 3px rail is the strongest brand cue available at
    this size, which is why each row carries one.

    THE ROW IS AN ANIMATION TARGET, and that is why it carries more markup than
    a score line needs:
      · data-game-slug / data-team-slug   — how the page finds the row that scored
      · --nfl-team (inline custom property) — the brand color the flash, the rail
                                              glow and the hot score all read, so
                                              the animation is the TEAM's color and
                                              not a generic grey
      · .nfl-row-wash                      — a pre-positioned overlay the flash
                                              fades in and out. Painting the row
                                              background directly would fight the
                                              text contrast; a separate layer at
                                              low opacity does not. %>
<% home = game.home_team %>
<% away = game.away_team %>
<div class="card p-0 overflow-hidden transition-shadow duration-300"
     data-game-slug="<%= game.slug %>"
     data-test="live-game-tile">
  <div class="divide-y divide-subtle">
    <% [[away, game.away_score], [home, game.home_score]].each do |team, score| %>
      <% color = team&.card_background || "#64748b" %>
      <%# The tile carries BOTH of a team's brand colors, because they do
          different jobs. `card_background` is the team's FIELD — right for a
          low-opacity wash behind a row. It is wrong for text: Pittsburgh's is
          #101820, which on this dark board is invisible, and the first cut of
          the hot score rendered exactly that. `card_mascot` is the ink that
          pairs with that field by construction (it is what the banner already
          uses over the same color), so it is what the score turns. %>
      <% ink = team&.card_mascot || "#e2e8f0" %>
      <div class="relative flex items-center gap-3 px-3 py-2.5 overflow-hidden"
           data-team-slug="<%= team&.slug %>"
           data-role="team-row"
           style="--nfl-team: <%= color %>; --nfl-team-ink: <%= ink %>">
        <span class="nfl-row-wash absolute inset-0 pointer-events-none opacity-0"
              style="background: <%= color %>"></span>
        <span class="nfl-rail relative w-1 h-8 rounded-full flex-shrink-0"
              style="background: <%= color %>"></span>
        <span class="relative text-xl leading-none flex-shrink-0"><%= team&.emoji %></span>
        <span class="relative min-w-0 flex-1">
          <span class="block text-sm font-bold text-heading truncate"><%= team&.short_name || team&.name %></span>
          <span class="block text-[11px] text-muted truncate"><%= team&.location %></span>
        </span>
        <span class="relative text-2xl font-extrabold text-heading tabular-nums transition-colors duration-700"
              data-role="score"><%= score.to_i %></span>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%# Status strip. `status_detail` is ESPN's own shortDetail ("Final",
      "Q3 8:42", "Halftime"), which says more than our three-state status
      column can — so it leads, and the column is only the fallback. %>
  <div class="px-3 py-1.5 bg-subtle/40 flex items-center justify-between gap-2">
    <% if game.live? %>
      <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1 text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-red-400">
        <span class="h-1.5 w-1.5 rounded-full bg-red-500 animate-pulse"></span> Live
      </span>
    <% elsif game.completed? %>
      <span class="text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted">Final</span>
    <% else %>
      <span class="text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-muted">
        <%= game.kickoff_at ? game.kickoff_at.strftime("%a %-I:%M %p") : "TBD" %>
      </span>
    <% end %>
    <span class="text-[11px] text-secondary font-mono truncate"><%= game.status_detail %></span>
  </div>
</div>
